            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186097817,
              ":tutorial/content": "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}\u2061x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}\u2061x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u206110}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}\u2061x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061a}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "7a7450e4-ec0e-5a5e-9c86-9430eb889be7",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2616,
